<Window x:Class="Bws.Gui.MainWindow"
        xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        xmlns:local="clr-namespace:Bws.Gui"
        Title="{DynamicResource gui.window.title}"
        Background="{DynamicResource ApplicationBackgroundBrush}"
        UseLayoutRounding="True"
        SnapsToDevicePixels="True"
        TextOptions.TextFormattingMode="Display"
        TextOptions.TextRenderingMode="ClearType"
        Height="700" Width="1100">

    <!--
        Every appearance value here points at a name in Themes/Values.xaml, and a guard in the
        architecture tests fails the build on one written in place - ADR-23. Height and Width
        are the window's own size, which is a one-off rather than something two screens can
        disagree about, so they are not policed.

        Text comes from the language file the same way, by key. A column header cannot bind
        to the view model: columns are not in the visual tree and inherit no data context,
        which is the trap this markup fell into on the first try.

        Background is set here rather than left alone, and it is a condition rather than a
        touch of polish: WPF UI's dictionaries do not give a plain Window a background, so
        without this line the window comes up white and THE WHOLE LIST DISAPPEARS - light text
        on a light surface - while the status line still reports that 811 entries were read.
        docs/10-WPF-UI.md, trap 2.

        THE FOUR RENDERING SETTINGS ARE MEASURED, NOT COPIED FROM AN ANSWER ON THE INTERNET.
        They arrived as a suggestion from the owner and were checked the way anything about
        pixels has to be: two builds differing only in these lines, the same text captured by
        PrintWindow from the release build, magnified nine times and compared. With them the
        strokes are thicker, whiter and land on the pixel grid. Without them the same glyphs
        are paler and sit at fractional positions.

        Measured AS A SET. Which of the four does the work was not separated, and saying so is
        cheaper than four more builds for an answer nobody needs yet. TextFormattingMode is the
        one with a known cost: Display mode is meant for small text and can look worse for
        large. This window has none, and that stops being true the day it gets a heading.
    -->

    <Grid Margin="{StaticResource PadPanel}">
        <Grid.RowDefinitions>
            <!--
                WHICH LIST THIS IS, and it stands above the question rather than beside it because
                the question is asked INSIDE it - owner's decision, 2026-08-19. Auto, like every
                other band over the list: what the window is for is the rows.
            -->
            <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />

            <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />

            <!--
                The filters, on their own row since 2026-08-11. Eight of them do not fit beside
                a search box that has to stay wide enough to read a query in, and A5 will grow
                this row rather than shrink it - account and signature are waiting on other
                slices. Auto, so the row costs nothing on a window too narrow to show it.
            -->
            <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />

            <!--
                WHAT CAN BE DONE TO THE ROWS SOMEBODY PICKED, since 2026-08-25 - backlog 224. Auto,
                like every other band over the list, and directly above the rows rather than at the
                top of the window: it acts on what is under it.
            -->
            <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />

            <RowDefinition Height="*" />
            <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />
        </Grid.RowDefinitions>

        <!--
            TWO COLUMNS SINCE 2026-08-13, and only the list row is divided. The question, the
            filters and what the window admits about the answer are about the whole listing, so
            they span both - the panel is about ONE entry and stands beside the rows rather than
            beside the search box. Auto, so a closed panel costs nothing at all.
        -->
        <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
            <ColumnDefinition Width="*" />
            <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
        </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>

        <!--
            WHICH LIST THE WINDOW IS SHOWING - services, drivers, or both. What it holds, why it is
            across the top rather than down the left, and why the positions are radio buttons is
            argued at the head of ScopeBar.xaml.
        -->
        <local:ScopeBar Grid.Row="0"
                        Grid.ColumnSpan="2"
                        x:Name="Scope"
                        Margin="{StaticResource MarginBelowSearch}" />

        <!--
            WHAT THE WINDOW IS ASKED, in its own file since the scope switch arrived - the field and
            the count that answers it. What it holds and why is argued at the head of SearchRow.xaml.

            IT MOVED BECAUSE THIS FILE WAS STANDING ON THE MARKUP CEILING, and that is worth saying
            plainly rather than dressing up: at 397 lines against a ceiling of 397, the next thing to
            go in needed something else to come out. The same thing happened to FilterRow on
            2026-08-13, and the seam was chosen the same way - by subject, not by line count.
        -->
        <local:SearchRow Grid.Row="1"
                         Grid.ColumnSpan="2"
                         x:Name="Search"
                         Margin="{StaticResource MarginBelowSearch}" />

        <!--
            HOW THE WINDOW ASKS, in its own file since 2026-08-13 - backlog 182 for the seam and 185
            for the shape. What it holds and why is argued at the head of FilterRow.xaml.

            THE SEAM IS A SUBJECT AND NOT A LINE COUNT: this row is how the window ASKS, and the
            rest of this file is what the window IS. The detail panel of `S7` needed room under the
            markup ceiling, and this was the block that was already about something else.

            A UserControl, exactly like the panel below - ONE mechanism for "a part of the window in
            its own file" rather than two. It spent a day as a style carrying a template in the
            theme, which cost a lookup by name, a refusal and a handler in the window for one
            button.
        -->
        <local:FilterRow Grid.Row="2"
                         Grid.ColumnSpan="2"
                         x:Name="Filters"
                         Margin="{StaticResource MarginBelowSearch}" />

        <!--
            Read only, and it stays that way. Every change to a service goes through a plan,
            so a cell somebody could type into would be a way to change one without ever
            producing a plan.

            Sorting arrived on 2026-08-10 and reordering with S6d2 the day after. What A10 has
            five rules about is answered the same way for both: the list reconciles itself in
            place and holds still while somebody is using it, so a sorted or rearranged list
            does not jump under a hand that is already on it.

            NO COLUMNS ARE DECLARED HERE ANY MORE. Seventeen of them are built in code from
            ViewModels/Columns.cs, because a list somebody chooses from cannot be a list written
            out - and the six that were here would have been a second copy of six of the
            seventeen. ListColumns.cs builds them and the two templated cells live in
            Themes/List.xaml, which is where a column built in code can reach them.

            IT SCROLLS SIDEWAYS SINCE 2026-08-12, reversing the refusal of 2026-08-05 - the
            owner's decision, taken once the window could show seventeen columns rather than
            six. Photographed with all seventeen on before it changed: nothing ran past the
            right edge and SEVEN COLUMNS SAT AT TWENTY PIXELS, each heading a single full stop
            over a column of sliced dots, with nothing on screen saying so.

            One word here is not the whole of it, and the rest is in ListColumns rather than
            repeated here: a scrollbar alone left those seven columns exactly as they were, and
            the first column is frozen by a count worked out from what is on screen rather than
            by the number one.
        -->
        <!--
            HOW THE LIST SCROLLS, AND BOTH ATTRIBUTES ARE ABOUT SMOOTHNESS RATHER THAN SPEED -
            owner's report, 2026-08-17, that dragging the scrollbar slowly feels like it snags.

            NOTHING ABOUT SCROLLING WAS SET BEFORE, so the grid ran on the registered defaults - and
            the timing probes could not see the problem at all. They measure how long the window
            takes to answer, and a jump that happens instantly is fast AND jerky. A latency
            instrument cannot report a smoothness fault, which is why three clean runs came first.

            ScrollUnit is the one that answers the complaint. A DataGrid scrolls by ITEM by default,
            so the viewport can only ever stop on a row boundary - at 811 entries in a viewport of
            about thirty, one pixel of thumb travel is worth several rows, and a slow drag therefore
            moves in visible jumps. Pixel lets it stop between boundaries. It is NOT the same as
            turning CanContentScroll off, which buys the same smoothness by disabling virtualisation
            and building all 811 rows - Microsoft's performance guidance lists that as one of the
            four ways to lose virtualisation without noticing.

            Recycling is the cheaper half. The default, Standard, creates a container as a row
            enters the viewport and throws it away as it leaves, so a drag down the list allocates
            and collects the whole way. Recycling reuses the containers instead.

            WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE: EnableColumnVirtualization. It is off by default, so
            turning it on is a real change rather than a no-op - but this grid sets
            FrozenColumnCount from code (ListColumns.cs) and those two are a known pair to get wrong
            together. It is a separate experiment with its own look, not a third attribute smuggled
            in beside two that are about scrolling.
        -->
        <!--
            THE ONLY VISIBLE WAY TO AN OPERATION, and until this line the only way at all was the
            right mouse button. What it holds, and why each button ends in three dots, is argued at
            the head of ActionBar.xaml.
        -->
        <local:ActionBar Grid.Row="3"
                         Grid.ColumnSpan="2"
                         x:Name="Actions"
                         Margin="{StaticResource MarginBelowSearch}" />

        <DataGrid Grid.Row="4"
                  x:Name="Entries"
                  MouseEnter="ListEngaged"
                  MouseLeave="ListReleased"
                  GotKeyboardFocus="ListEngaged"
                  LostKeyboardFocus="ListReleased"
                  ItemsSource="{Binding Rows}"
                  Style="{StaticResource ListGrid}"
                  RowStyle="{StaticResource ListRow}"
                  CellStyle="{StaticResource ListCell}"
                  ColumnHeaderStyle="{StaticResource ColumnHeading}"
                  AutoGenerateColumns="False"
                  IsReadOnly="True"
                  CanUserSortColumns="True"
                  CanUserReorderColumns="True"
                  CanUserAddRows="False"
                  GridLinesVisibility="Horizontal"
                  HorizontalGridLinesBrush="{StaticResource SurfaceRowLine}"
                  HeadersVisibility="Column"
                  HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Auto"
                  VirtualizingPanel.ScrollUnit="Pixel"
                  VirtualizingPanel.VirtualizationMode="Recycling"
                  SelectionUnit="FullRow"
                  SelectionMode="Extended"
                  PreviewMouseRightButtonDown="PointAtRowBeforeMenu"
                  ContextMenuOpening="OfferTheMenuOnlyOnARow">

            <!--
                MORE THAN ONE ROW SINCE 2026-08-18, packet 2 of `S7`. `C2` asks for Shift and Ctrl
                beside an action, and the operations that use it arrive in this packet.

                NOTHING IN THIS WINDOW BINDS TO THE SELECTION and that is a repair rather than an
                omission - the argument is at Copy in the code behind. The selection is read at the
                moment somebody asks for something, because a binding into a list that reconciles
                itself once a second is another party in the middle of `A10`.

                One consequence lives in the code behind rather than here, and it is the one that
                would otherwise be a silent wrong answer: a right click on a row OUTSIDE the
                selection has to replace it, because setting a row selected only ever adds.
            -->

            <!--
                READ ONLY, LIKE THE LIST. Everything that changes a service goes through a plan,
                so a menu item that stopped one from here would be an operation without a plan -
                rule 1 of the project notes, and not a temporary shortcut either. Those arrive
                with the screen that can show a plan.

                It hangs on the grid rather than on the row style, and that is the difference
                between a menu and a menu that works: a ContextMenu in a resource dictionary has
                nowhere to put a handler. The grid is also an ancestor of whatever cell has focus,
                so the menu key opens it without a second copy for the keyboard.
            -->
            <DataGrid.ContextMenu>
                <ContextMenu>
                    <MenuItem Header="{DynamicResource gui.menu.copyName}" Click="CopyServiceName" />
                    <MenuItem Header="{DynamicResource gui.menu.copyDisplayName}" Click="CopyDisplayName" />
                    <MenuItem Header="{DynamicResource gui.menu.copyDescription}" Click="CopyDescription" />

                    <!--
                        THE WHOLE ENTRY, AND IT IS WHAT Ctrl+C DOES TOO - owner's request,
                        2026-08-13. Everything the catalogue knows rather than the columns that
                        happen to be on, because somebody copying an entry into a ticket wants what
                        there is to know and the twelve columns that are off by default are exactly
                        the ones too long to have kept on screen.
                    -->
                    <MenuItem Header="{DynamicResource gui.menu.copyAll}" Click="CopyEverything" />

                    <Separator />

                    <!--
                        WORDED AS QUESTIONS RATHER THAN AS VERBS, AND THEY STAYED THAT WAY WHEN THE
                        RUNNER ARRIVED ON 2026-08-19. These still open a plan and nothing else, so an
                        item called "Stop" would still be a lie to somebody's hand - what changed is
                        that the plan now has a button under it. Items that acted straight from here
                        would be a second road to a write, and the one thing `docs/11` 9.2 refuses is
                        asking about an effect nobody has been shown.
                    -->
                    <MenuItem Header="{DynamicResource gui.menu.previewStop}" Click="PreviewStop" />
                    <MenuItem Header="{DynamicResource gui.menu.previewStart}" Click="PreviewStart" />
                    <MenuItem Header="{DynamicResource gui.menu.previewRestart}" Click="PreviewRestart" />
                </ContextMenu>
            </DataGrid.ContextMenu>

        </DataGrid>

        <!--
            WHAT THE MIDDLE OF THE WINDOW SAYS WHEN IT HAS NO ROWS, in its own file since
            2026-08-18. Complaint 9 of the eleven. Why it sits OVER the grid rather than instead
            of it, why it takes no clicks, and why five states are worked out in ListState rather
            than in markup are all at the head of EmptyState.xaml.

            IT LEFT THIS FILE BECAUSE THIS FILE HAD NOWHERE LEFT TO GO, and the number is worth
            keeping: MainWindow stood at 403 lines against a markup ceiling of 403, which may only
            ever go down. The packet that puts operations in this window could not have added one
            line here. Same wall the details panel met on 2026-08-13, same answer.

            Bound to what the window SAYS rather than to the whole model, so nothing in it can
            reach the list, the query or the reading.
        -->
        <local:EmptyState Grid.Row="4" x:Name="ListEmptyState" DataContext="{Binding Says}" />

        <!--
            EVERYTHING ABOUT ONE ENTRY, `S7`. Opened with Enter from the list and closed with
            Escape - which is the last of the three keys `docs/11` 9.1 asked for on 2026-08-05 and
            the one that waited for a screen to open, backlog 59.

            Beside the list rather than under it, because the list is what the window is for and a
            panel across the bottom would take rows away from every window height. Its own file,
            because it is a part of the window rather than the look of a control - the reasoning is
            at the head of DetailsView.xaml.

            Bound to the chosen entry rather than to the whole model, so nothing in the panel can
            reach the list, the query or the reading. What it may say is exactly what Chosen holds.
        -->
        <local:DetailsView Grid.Row="4"
                           Grid.Column="1"
                           x:Name="DetailsPanel"
                           DataContext="{Binding Chosen}" />

        <!--
            WHAT AN OPERATION OVER THE SELECTION WOULD DO, in the SAME CELL as the details panel and
            mutually exclusive with it - the window closes one when it opens the other. Why that is
            right, and why this file is a UserControl, are at the head of PlanView.xaml.
        -->
        <local:PlanView Grid.Row="4"
                        Grid.Column="1"
                        x:Name="PlanPanel"
                        DataContext="{Binding Planned}" />

        <!--
            WHAT THE ANSWER ADMITS ABOUT ITSELF, and the one way out this window offers. Its own
            file since 2026-08-25, for the reason at the head of StatusRow.xaml - the fourth part
            of this window to leave the same way, after the scope switch, the search row and the
            filters row.

            The elevate button's click is wired in the constructor rather than in the markup over
            there, because pressing it starts a process and Elevation.cs is the only file in this
            product allowed to name one.
        -->
        <local:StatusRow Grid.Row="5" Grid.ColumnSpan="2" x:Name="Status" />
    </Grid>
</Window>
